Move the echo canceller's filters with the buffer when it goes to an echo sooner than the headroom

Patch 0017 moves the render buffer to delay 0 once 125 lags in a row put the echo sooner
than the delay headroom and before where the buffer stands. Each such move was taken as a
new delay: the echo remover started its filters, ERLE and initial state over
(kNewDetectedDelay), although the echo path had not changed, only where the buffer
stands in front of it. And the estimate of 0 counted as a steady estimate, so the
estimator reset itself every half second while the short lags went on. After an unmute
(the recording restarted, which puts the buffer back where its reset puts it) the buffer
moved again, the filters started over again, and the words after the unmute went out
weaker than with main: in the simulator, with an echo of 2 to 30 ms, 1.6 to 1.9 dB on
average over 288 runs, a third of the words 1 dB or more and one in six 3 dB or more.

A move for lags sooner than the headroom now moves both filters (and the filter
analyzer's view of them) by the blocks the buffer moved, instead of starting them over;
what they learnt of the echo stays where the echo is. When the buffer later leaves that
delay without a delay found (a reset at a restart of the recording, a flush, an
underrun), the filters are moved back with it. The buffer goes to one block before the
echo (the last short lag, in blocks, less one), not to 0, so that the filters keep as
much of the echo's start as they can, and never above where it stood before the first
such move. A delay from short lags neither counts as a steady estimate nor resets the
estimator, so the histogram goes on and still finds an echo the buffer can reach.
AecState does not take that delay as an external delay: the buffer then stands at the
echo itself, not the headroom before it, so the filter's own delay is used.

Making the filters longer (8 blocks more, as the headroom) brought the words back to main
too, but a change of output in a call to a nearer echo (30 to 2 ms) then let through 6 dB
more echo, as the longer filter lowers the reverberation's estimate; keeping that
estimate on the filter's usual length lost the words again (-0.36 dB). Dropped.

Simulator (M158 with this branch's patches, AEC3 alone at 16 kHz, headroom 512, speech-
like far and near ends, room responses; the same seeds for main, this branch before and
after), against main, words in dB (mean over all words) and echo after the unmute or the
change (runs with 1 dB or more of it than main):
- unmute 6 s after a change of output, echo 2 to 30 ms (288 runs, 5184 words): words -1.90
  and -1.58 before, -0.16 and -0.11 now; more echo in 49 runs before, 2 now (up to 1.8 dB);
- normal delays (20 to 200 ms, 84 runs): as main (before: -0.03 dB, 3 runs with more echo);
- a change of output without mute or restart (96 runs): words -0.27, less echo in 70 runs,
  more in none;
- a change of output in a call, echo 2 to 180 ms (54 runs): words -0.09, more echo in 9
  (before: 6); 30 to 2 ms and back (72 runs): words -0.35, more echo in 14, less in 22.
  With main's code run twice the same way (dither), the last grid moves in 9 runs and
  words by 0.01 dB: most of these 14 are noise, the rest are the nearer echo now within
  the filter's reach, cancelled as AEC3 cancels any echo it reaches.
Unit tests: a filter's partitions moved both ways, the ones left behind cleared; a move
to an echo sooner than the headroom shifts the filters instead of starting them again,
shifts them back when the buffer leaves that delay without a new delay found, and any
other new delay still starts them again.

